=== SURVEY ===
Web source VehiclePicker.tsx is the persistent app-wide vehicle selector mounted in the sidebar header. It
composes three hooks:
  • useTranslation         — the one `t('vehiclePicker.aria', 'Select vehicle')` accessible-name key.
  • useSelectedVehicle()   — `vehicles` (the fleet), `vehicleId` (current selection), `setVehicleId` (commit).
  • usePinned('vehicle')   — the user's vehicle pins (`item_id` + `position`).
Behaviour:
  • sorted   : if pins.length === 0 → vehicles as-is; else a STABLE sort floating pinned vehicles to the top
               in ascending `position`, the rest in original fleet order (the `return 0` branch relies on
               Array.prototype.sort stability).
  • options  : sorted.map → { value: String(v.id), label: pinned ? `📌 ${base}` : base } where
               base = v.display_name || v.vin || `Vehicle ${v.id}` (JS `||` falsy chain).
  • gate     : `if (vehicles.length <= 1) return null` — the picker hides itself for a 0/1-vehicle account.
  • render   : a leading `Car` glyph + a `<Select>` whose value is `String(vehicleId)`, onChange →
               setVehicleId(Number(value) when finite & > 0, else null).
Shared components used: @/components/ui (Select). Charts/maps: none. i18n keys extracted: `vehiclePicker.aria`
("Select vehicle"). Existing native: no VehiclePicker* symbols (no collision).

=== REASONING ===
- The web is a presentational selector that hides for ≤1 vehicle; the prompt requires a production-polished
  native surface reproducing its DATA (fleet + pins + selection), composition, states, and i18n, with EVERY
  state visible (no blank box). Chosen: a HIG-idiomatic `Menu` picker for multi-vehicle fleets (label = the
  bordered selector chip with the current vehicle name + a pin glyph when the selection is pinned + a selector
  chevron; items = one Button per vehicle in pin-aware order, pinned rows pin-marked, the current one
  checkmarked), routing a pick back through the model's host-supplied `onSelect` (the native peer of
  `setVehicleId`). This mirrors the predecessor ActiveVehicleSegment(0176) switcher exactly, adapted from
  metrics/units to pins.
- The web `vehicles.length <= 1 → null` becomes the native always-render leaf split (P4 forbids blank boxes,
  per the 0176 precedent which also returns null in web): fleet == 1 → a non-interactive static chip naming
  the single vehicle; fleet == 0 → a friendly "No vehicles" chip; plus loading skeleton + error retry tile +
  the orthogonal stale/offline freshness chip.
- Architecture (per-surface self-contained types, no SwiftUI in the pure core):
  • Adapter (pure, Foundation): VehiclePickerSurface.slug, the Resolve facade seam, the value types
    (Vehicle / Pin / Option / Projection / Copy / Connection), and VehiclePickerProjector — the VERBATIM port
    of the web name chain, the STABLE pin-aware comparator, the option map, and the projection (incl. the
    `isPickable = vehicles.count >= 2` single-vs-picker decision).
  • Seams: VehiclePickerTelemetry (P1/S11 view.opened) + OSLog default; Phase; Snapshot (the composed
    useSelectedVehicle + usePinned value + loading/error/connection); Source protocol + Live + InMemory.
  • Model: VehiclePickerStrings i18n facade (the verbatim `vehiclePicker.aria` + `vehiclePicker.fallback`
    for the web-hardcoded `Vehicle ${id}` + native chrome) + @Observable VehiclePickerModel (ingest → phase,
    derive projection, route select, stale-auto-refresh-once, view.opened once).
  • View/Views + Previews + .strings + Tests/AdapterTests.
- The web `📌` label prefix → a native `pin.fill` SF Symbol + a "Pinned" VoiceOver hint (more HIG-idiomatic +
  accessible than an emoji in body text), carried as the Option.isPinned flag so the data is preserved.
- The web hardcodes `Vehicle ${id}` (no t()) → routed through `vehiclePicker.fallback` ("Vehicle") so the
  native code holds no English literal while rendering the identical string (no parity loss).
- Rejected: a literally-hidden surface for ≤1 vehicle (P4 leaf contract forbids blank boxes — rendered a
  static chip / empty chip); HTTP in the view (bound via the source seam); an unstable Swift `sorted` (used an
  index-decorated stable sort to match JS sort stability); reusing ActiveVehicleSegment's types (repo
  convention is per-surface self-contained types).

